\section{Conclusions \& Limitations}\label{sec:conclusion&limitations}
In this paper, we present CorePipe, a fully automated pipeline to generate high-quality, diverse, and controllable repository-level benchmark test cases, and introduce \textsc{CoreCodeBench}, a configurable benchmark that comprehensively evaluates  capabilities of LLMs in real-world engineering scenarios. Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that \textsc{CoreCodeBench} enables both coarse- and fine-grained analysis of LLMs' coding abilities, revealing significant performance differences across various tasks and highlighting areas where current models still fall short, especially in complex and multi-function engineering contexts. Our work provides a scalable and rigorous testbed for the systematic assessment and future improvement of LLMs in engineering-level code development, paving the way for more robust and adaptable AI-driven software engineering tools.

Despite the automated generation of six types of questions from GitHub repositories achieved by CorePipe, our pipeline currently relies on the presence of comprehensive unit tests within the repositories. Repositories lacking sufficient unit tests cannot be processed by our current framework. In future work, we plan to enhance CorePipe by incorporating techniques for generating or augmenting unit tests, thereby expanding its applicability to a broader range of projects. Additionally, \textsc{CoreCodeBench} currently focuses exclusively on Python repositories. We aim to extend support to other major programming languages, such as Java and C++, to enable more comprehensive evaluation of engineering capabilities.


