\begin{abstract}
\bigskip

\noindent
We explore the impact of a combined analysis of $B_{d,s}\to K^{(*)}$ form factors
on a set of $L$-observables. The $L$-observables are constructed from ratios of branching fractions in $B_{s}\to VV,PP,PV$ versus $B_d \to VV,PP,PV$ decays with $P=K^0,\bar{K}^0$ and $V=K^{*0},\bar{K}^{*0}$,  thereby partially reducing their hadronic uncertainties.
We show the change of the Standard Model predictions of the $L$-observables under different determinations of the ratio of the relevant form factors (with correlations) including lattice QCD data and a novel light-cone sum rule analysis. 
In addition, we provide precise results for all $B_{d,s} \to K^{(*)}$ form factors in machine-readable files.
We find that the inclusion of our up-to-date results, as well as the use or omission of lattice QCD data for the form factors, has a significant impact on the $L$-observables.  
We also discuss how the New Physics interpretation is affected by the updated form factors and present revised predictions for the mechanism identified in our analysis of $B \to VP$ decays, now employing more suitable new experimental observables defined in this paper. 


\end{abstract}
