The observables with an optimised NP sensitivity can be separated in two cases depending on the meson that collects the spectator quark. 
{\bf Notice that these observables may require the knowledge of the crossed correlations between $B_{d,s}\to K^*$ with $B_{d,s} \to K$.}



\subsection{Soft form factors estimate}
\medskip

Naively, one can do a very simple exercise consisting in evaluating the ratio of form factors in the limit of the initial hadron ($B_d$ or $B_s$) being heavy and the maximal recoil for the final $K^*$, neglecting terms of order $1/m_b$ and $\alpha_s$ \cite{charles}. In this limit one find for the $B_{d,s} \to K^*$ system:
\begin{equation}
r_V(q^2=0)=\frac{A_0^{B_s \to K^*}(q^2=0)}{A_0^{B_d \to K^*}(q^2=0)}\simeq 
\frac{m_{B_s}}{m_{B_d}} \frac{\xi_{\|}^{B_s \to K^*}(E_{K^*})}{\xi_{\|}^{B_d \to K^*}(E_{K^*})}
\end{equation}
By inverting this ratio one finds using determination II  for the form factors (LCSR+lattice) that the ratio of soft form factors are equal at the per mille level. Instead, when using determination I (LCSR) the ratio of soft form factors differ by a significant 16\%. This large difference would require that the $\alpha_s$ corrections not included in this naive estimate exhibit a high sensitivity on the decaying initial hadron being a $B_d$ or a $B_s$ or conversely, that determination II is favored.

In a similar way for the ratio of form factors $f_0^{B_s \to K}$ in the $B_{d,s}\to K$ system one finds \cite{Beneke:2000wa}:
\begin{equation}
r_P(q^2)=\frac{f_0^{B_s \to K}}{f_0^{B_d \to K}}\simeq \frac{m_{B_s}}{m_{B_d}} \frac{\xi_P^{B_s \to 
K}}{\xi_P^{B_d \to 
K}}
\end{equation}
In this case, on the contrary, the determination I for the form factors (LCSR) is the one that implies that the ratio of soft form factors are equal at the 2\% level while the second determination II (LCSR+lattice) in this case implies a 19\% difference in the ratio. Here same conclusion applies but favoring determination I if the large difference cannot be attributed to a large initial hadron dependent $\alpha_s$ contribution.

If one takes determination II for $B \to K^*$ and determination I for $B \to K$ then $L_{\bar{K}^*K^*} \simeq L_{KK}$.

%For $A_0^{B_{d,s}\to K^*}$, we can use LCSR results at maximum recoil from ref.~\cite{Beneke:2000wa}. If one uses LCSR+Lattice results from the same paper, a naive central value analysis neglecting annihilation contributions yields $23.76$ for $L_{K^*\bar{K}^*}$.....

