    \label{fig:ap_asfr_dvscifar10}
\end{figure*}
\begin{figure*}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{./asfr/ncaltech101.pdf}
    \caption{Visualization of ASFR in VGGSNN on N-Caltech101. Similar to the results on DVS-CIFAR10, TRT significantly reduced the ASFR of each layer in SNN.}
    \label{fig:ap_asfr_ncaltech101}
\end{figure*}
\subsection{Training Detail}
In this paper, all experiments are based on the PyTorch framework and running on both Nvidia RTX A100, 4090 GPUs. The N-Caltech101 dataset is loaded via SpikingJelly\footnote{https://github.com/fangwei123456/spikingjelly}. Table \sref{tab:ap_hyperparameter} gives the hyperparameter settings for the experiments. In TRT, $\lambda$ controls the initial strength of the regular term and $\delta$ controls the decay rate, the trend of regularization intensity over time is jointly influenced by these two hyperparameters. Table \ref{tab:different_delta} shows the effect of different $\delta$ values. Furthermore, we find that combining a high $\lambda$ and a low $\delta$ can harm the mid-term training stages. As a result, we suggest using 0.25 as the base value for $\delta$, which should be adjusted based on the magnitude of $\lambda$. 
% \subsection{Experimental Results on CIFAR Datasets}
% Table \sref{tab:ap_comparison} shows the complete experimental results of state-of-the-art methods on CIFAR10 and CIFAR100. As demostrated in Table \sref{tab:ap_comparison}, our proposed TRT method outperforms existing works on both CIFAR10 and CIFAR100 datasets. On CIFAR10, our method achieves the highest accuracy among all existing approaches. Even in the simulation length setting $T=2$, our TRT method still has increments of $2.14\%$ and $1.22\%$ compared to STBP-tdBN and TET with $T=6$, respectively. On CIFAR100, TRT also achieves superior performance across all simulation length settings, surpassing state-of-the-art methods.
\subsection{Average Spike Firing Rate Visualization}
The ASFR of VGGSNN on DVS-CIFAR10 and N-Caltech101 are shown in Fig. \sref{fig:ap_asfr_dvscifar10} and Fig. \sref{fig:ap_asfr_ncaltech101}, respectively. On DVS-CIFAR10, TRT reduces ASFR by $40.83\%$ to $60.61\%$ across layers. Similar to on CIFAR10-DVS, the model trained from TRT on N-Caltech101 also has a lower ASFR compared to SDT. These results indicate that our TRT method significantly reduces the ASFR while achieving better performance than the SDT method. Therefore, our method reduces the energy consumption of SNNs, making it suitable for training high-performance models deployed on edge devices.

