We end this work by an {\sl erratum} in the paper \cite{BGS1} concerning the boundedness of Hankel operators on $A^1(\C_+)$.
 Recall that the Hankel operator $h_b$ is defined  by $h_b(f)=P\left(f\Bar{b}\right)$ when this makes sense. Equivalently, for $b$ a Bloch function, $h_b$ is defined as an operator from $A^2(\C_+)$ into itself as the operator satisfying $\langle h_b(f),g\rangle =\langle b,fg\rangle $ for any $f,g\in A^2(\C_+)$. Here $\langle \cdot,\cdot\rangle$ stands for the usual Hermitian inner product on $L^2(\C_+)$.\\
 We want to extend this operator as an operator from $A^1(\C_+)$ to itself. It is natural to consider  quantities such as $\langle b,fg\rangle $ for $f\in A^1(\C_+)$ and $g$ an element of the Bloch space. However, as $g$ is defined in the Bloch space up to a constant, in order to give a meaning to this term, one has to assume that $\langle b,f\rangle =0$. We forgot to add this condition in our previous paper \cite{BGS1}. Our corrected statement is the following.
 %{\color{blue}Our first remark is the fact that the Hankel operator cannot be bounded on the whole  space $A_{\omega^l}^1(\mathbb{C}_+)$. Indeed, we have the following proposition, which is related to
 %the condition \eqref{meanB}.
% \begin{proposition}\label{hankelmean}


% \end{proposition}

We define the space $A^1_{b}(\C_+)$ as the subspace of functions $f$ in $A^1(\C_+)$ satisfying $\langle b,f \rangle=0$.
\begin{theorem}\label{hankelexp}
Let $b$ be in $\mathcal H(\C_+)$.  Then the Hankel operator $h_b$ extends to a bounded operator from $A^1_{b}(\mathbb{C}_+)$ to $A^1(\mathbb{C}_+)$ if and only if $b$ belongs to $\mathcal{B}_\omega(\mathbb{C}_+)$.
Moreover, when $f\in A^1(\C_+)$ and $\langle b,f\rangle \neq 0$, $h_b(f)\notin L^1(\C_+)$.
\end{theorem}
