\subsection{\ourMethod Algorithm}
\label{sec:mainalgorithm}



%\ourMethod aims to train a policy for deployment in an unknown target domain $E_t$ without direct interactions in that environment.
%Our goal of \ourMethod ~is to deploy a policy in an unknown target domain $E_t$ without direct interactions in the environment.
%Instead of fine-tuning or validating a policy in the target domain, which brings safety concerns, 
%We assume that we have access to a limited target domain dataset $\dataset_t$, %which captures the difference between the target domain and the simulated source domain. 
%where $\dataset_t$ could act as a proxy for the target domain $E_t$, allowing us to evaluate policy performance on $E_t$ without the risk of unsafe deployments.
%Its quality and diversity, including edge cases and failure modes, are crucial for deployment success.



\ourMethod builds upon a curriculum of DR.
Starting from an initial environment $E_0$, we introduce a repulsive environment $E_1$ through parameter randomization and gradually expand the randomization range. % to create a curriculum of increasingly diverse scenarios. 
This progression nudges the agent's exploration towards the target environment $E_t$ (\autoref{fig:exploration}). Importantly, $E_{t'}$ represents a subset of $E_t$ sufficient for effective policy performance. Focusing on $E_{t'}$ balances comprehensive scenario coverage and computational efficiency, avoiding the exhaustive exploration of DR. 



Initially, we execute a behavior policy for $n$ episodes in an unaltered simulation environment $E_0$, generating an offline dataset $\dataset_0$. We then introduce slight randomization to a single parameter in the environment to reach to $E_1$, collecting a repulsive dataset $\dataset_1$ using the same behavior policy. These two datasets, $\dataset_0$ (nominal) and $\dataset_1$ (repulsive), are used in our loss function in \autoref{eq:our_loss}: $\dataset_0$ contributes to the standard RL loss, while $\dataset_1$ informs the diversity term $\mathcal{L}^{\text{RL}}_{\text{div}}$.

We detect OOD scenario through the critics' variance on $\dataset_t$. 
Low ensemble variance indicates $\dataset_t$ is aligned with the nominal dataset ($\dataset_0$) and high variance suggests a deviation from it. %$\dataset_t$ is aligned with the repulsive dataset ($\dataset_1$). 
%Trained via \autoref{eq:our_loss} to achieve consensus on ID data ($\dataset$) while maintaining diverse perspectives on OOD scenarios ($\dataset'$),  ID alignment while high variance signals OOD states. 
%By comparing this variance to an adaptively selected threshold $\tau$ (\autoref{sec:exp_uncertainty}), we determine if the policy will reliably generalize to the target environment $E_t$. 
When the uncertainty measure falls below a threshold $\tau$ (\autoref{sec:exp_uncertainty}), $\dataset_t$ is considered sufficiently aligned with $E_0$, and the policy is deemed ready for deployment. 


