\section{Introduction}
Measurement of the time of arrival of Minimum Ionizing Particles (MIPs) is now recognized as an important tool to
disentangle individual collisions in high pile-up environments, such as those found at the high-luminosity LHC or at the future FCC-hh
collider.

\begin{comment}

}This is exemplified by the development of subdetector systems with a time resolution in the range 30-\SI{50}{\pico s} for
the high luminosity data taking
phase of the LHC experiments, like the High Granularity Timing Detector (HGTD) of ATLAS, or the High Granularity
Calorimeter (HGCAL) and Mip Timing Detector (MTD) for CMS.
For these projects where actual data taking should start by 2030, the chosen sensor technology is the Low
Gain Avalanche Detector technology, given its proven ability to reach the required timing performance, as well as required radiation tolerance. 
%In addition,
%thorough R\&D has brought the radiation tolerance to %the level required for high luminosity LHC operation.
\end{comment}

On the longer term, other technologies than the presently largely used LGADs have to be investigated, to drive down the cost of sub detector systems 
potentially featuring hundreds of square meters of sensor surface.
\begin{comment}
This is already true for FCC-ee, where timing
performance at the same level is required as at the high luminosity LHC for Time of Flight measurements and particle
identification.
\end{comment}
Measurement of the time of arrival of charged particles with a resolution of the
order of \SI{20}{\pico s} is also one of the strategic goals of the CERN DRD3 collaboration.

One very promising technology to reach this goal is High Voltage High Resistivity CMOS ~\cite{hv-cmos}. This kind of sensor has the advantage
of being relatively cheap, since it is based on high production volume industrial processes. It is technically
possible to integrate on the same substrate the sensor diode, the analog front-end processing electronics, shaper and
discriminator, and at least part of the digital processing electronics, amplitude, time of arrival measurement and data
serialisation. With such a sensor, there is no need for costly bump bonding operations.

\section{Sensor design}
\begin{comment}
\subsection{Contributions to time resolution}
There are several key contributions to the time resolution of a silicon sensor. The first one is the jitter term,
equal to the ratio between the total signal over noise ratio (SNR) and the signal risetime. To minimize this contribution, one has to keep the
noise as low as possible, and at the same time have a risetime as short as possible. The noise depends upon the technology chosen,
and also upon the sensor capacitance, small capacitances and small pixels leading to the best potential performance. For a non amplified
sensor, the risetime depends mostly upon the electric field applied to the sensor, and saturates at a value of a few ns for an
electric field of about \SI{1}{V}/\SI{}{\micro m}. For a typical signal over noise ratio of about 100 and a risetime of a few ns, this contribution is a few tens of picoseconds.
The second contribution to the time resolution comes from the pulse to pulse amplitude variations. These can to a large
extent be corrected offline by the application of a time-walk correction, i.e. correcting the time of arrival of each
particle by a function of the amplitude of the signal. There is however an irreducible contribution, coming from the 
fluctuations of the energy deposits along the path of the charged particle in the sensor depth. The only way to reduce this
contribution is to reduce the sensor thickness. The third contribution is related
to the weighthing field uniformity. If the geometry of the field lines depends upon the impact position of the incident particles,
the shape of the signal and hence the measurement of the time of arrival depend in a complicated way on the impact position.
To minimize this third contribution, it is important to have an electric field geometry
where the sensor electrodes are as close as possible to a parallel plate geometry. This calls for pixels with lateral
dimensions that are typically larger than the thickness of the depleted region.
\end{comment}
\begin{comment}
This kind of design is often called
large-size electrodes. Small size electrodes, where the dimensions of the collecting electrodes are significantly smaller
than the sensor thickness and pixel dimensions allows sensors with small pixels, low power consumption, and is
very appealing for tracking sensors.
\end{comment}

\subsection{Design compromises}
Designing a non amplified monolithic timing sensor involves finding compromises on several parameters.
Large collecting electrode dimensions are favorable for field uniformity, but give higher noise. Thin sensors allow to
reduce the influence of the energy deposit fluctuations along the particle path, but reduce the signal over noise ratio, which increases
the jitter contribution (equal to the signal over noise divided by the rise time) to the total time resolution. The analog front-end electronics needs to have an 
impulse response with a rise time comparable or lower than the rise time of the physics current signal, which is a few ns. Achieving this typically means high bias currents and hence high power dissipation in the analog front-end. Power dissipation of several Watts per square centimeter is not a problem for small scale
demonstrators, but practical sensor implementation in actual experiments has to be compatible
with the available cooling power, which is at most a few hundreds of \SI{}{\milli W} per square centimeter. 
%The higher
%the front-end density, and the smaller the %pixel size, the higher the power dissipation %per surface unit.

\subsection{MiniCactus V2 sensor design}   
MiniCactus V2 is a timing sensor intended to explore the compromises outlined in the previous section, and
to investigate the timing performance that can be reached with a large-electrode
sensor design implemented with a high-voltage high resistivity process, without intrinsic amplification of the collected
charge, in contrast to LGADs. MiniCactus V2 is also an improvement over the previous MiniCactus V1 chip, that had the
same goal. MiniCactus V1 test-beam results have been published in~\cite{minicactus_v1}. The best timing performance reached by MiniCactus V1 is a
65 ps time resolution on MIPs (180 GeV/c muons at the CERN H4 beamline), with a \SI{200}{\micro m} thick chip, on a
\SI{500}{\micro m} $\times$ \SI{1000}{\micro m} pixel operated at a bias voltage of 450 V~\cite{minicactus_v1}.

The technology used for MiniCactus V1 and MiniCactus V2 is the LFoundry LF15A 150 nm process. This analog and digital mixed signal
technology provides up to six metal
layers, and the foundry gives the possibility to use High Voltage High Resistivity (2 k $\Omega$cm) wafers. After fabrication, the wafers
have to be thinned to the desired thickness. The backside of the wafers is then doped with Boron and metallized,
before finally being diced. Chips from wafers thinned down to 150, 175, and \SI{200}{\micro m} are available. MiniCactus V1 results suggest
the optimal thickness should lie in this range.

The typical architecture of the front-end and discriminator electronics attached to each sensor diode is shown in figure~\ref{FE}.
MiniCactus V1 uses only a charge sensitive preamplifier as analog front-end. This block, called CSA1, also used by MiniCactus V2, is an evolution of the preamplifier used
by the LF-CPIX chip~\cite{LF-CPIX1,LF-CPIX2}, after optimization for time measurement.
