\section{CXL-aware Long-Context LLM Fine-Tuning}\label{sec:design}
To curb the latency hit of CXL while still exploiting its capacity, the proposed CXL-aware long-context LLM fine-tuning first introduces the CXL‑aware memory allocation to keep latency‑critical optimizer data in DRAM and relegates bulky, latency‑tolerant tensors to CXL memory (See Section~\ref{sec:design:allocation}). Complementing this, Multi‑AIC striping spreads each GPU’s CXL‑resident data across multiple add‑in cards, eliminating single-link contention and pooling bandwidth, an approach that also scales when optimizer states spill beyond DRAM (See Section~\ref{sec:design:striping}).

\subsection{CXL-aware Memory Allocation}\label{sec:design:allocation}
Building on the previous observations in Section~\ref{sec:analysis:cpu_compute}, the CXL‑aware memory allocation is introduced to reserve local DRAM for the data most sensitive to latency during the CPU‑based optimizer step, namely the full‑precision model parameters $P$, gradients $G$, and optimizer states $O$. Data that tolerates higher latency, such as checkpointed activations $A$ and \texttt{bf16} copies of parameters and gradients streamed to the GPU, is directed to CXL‑attached memory. This selective placement harnesses the greater capacity of CXL memory for storing latency‑tolerant information without suffering the latency penalty of CXL-attached memory when latency‑critical optimizer data reside on slower medium. Aligning each data type with the memory tier that best matches its access pattern not only improves efficiency but also enhances scalability for long‑context workloads. As the context length or batch size expands, the footprint of the activation checkpoints $A$ increases substantially (see Section~\ref{sec:back:memory_scale}), while the sizes of $P$, $G$, and $O$ remain fixed relative to the model parameters. Offloading the ever‑increasing activation data to the expandable CXL pool alleviates pressure on local DRAM, enabling a stable DRAM configuration while relying on CXL for capacity growth. Figure~\ref{fig:method_example}(a) visualizes the memory layout, highlighting how each data component is distributed between local DRAM and CXL‑attached memory.% under the proposed strategy.

%Using these observations in ~\ref{sec:analysis}, we propose a \textbf{CXL-aware Memory Allocation} strategy. This strategy prioritizes placing the data required for the latency-sensitive CPU-based optimizer step, specifically, the full-precision model parameters $P$, gradients $G$ and optimizer states $O$ in local DRAM. Concurrently, it allocates the more latency-tolerant data involved primarily in GPU data transfers, such as checkpointed activations $A$ and the bf16 copies of parameters and gradients intended for GPU computation to the CXL-attached memory.

%The primary advantage of this approach is that it exploits the larger capacity offered by CXL-attached memory for storing latency tolerance data, like activation checkpoints, without incurring the severe performance penalty identified in Section~\ref{sec:analysis:cpu_compute} that arises from placing latency-critical optimizer data on the slower CXL-attached Memory. This selective placement aligns memory usage with the distinct access patterns and performance characteristics of local DRAM versus CXL-attached memory. Furthermore, this strategy intrinsically supports scalability for long-context scenarios. As context length or batch size increases, the memory required for activation checkpoints $A$ grows substantially (Section~\ref{sec:back:memory_scale}), whereas the size of the data related to the optimizer step (full-precision $P$, $G$, $O$) remains static relative to the model size. By allocating the dynamically growing activation data to the expandable CXL-attached memory, the demands on local DRAM associated with context scaling are mitigated, potentially allowing systems to maintain a stable local DRAM configuration while leveraging CXL for expansion.

%An illustration of this core CXL-aware Memory Allocation strategy is depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:method_example}(a), detailing the placement of different data components across local DRAM and CXL-attached memory.

\subsection{Multi-AIC Striping}\label{sec:design:striping}
Even though CXL AICs make it straightforward to expand system memory capacity, effectively using multiple cards is still challenging. Earlier observations in Section~\ref{sec:analysis:gpu_transfer} reveal severe bandwidth contention whenever several GPUs tried to access the same AIC. Additional profiling also showed that latency‑tolerant transfer data—checkpointed activations $A$, parameters $P$, and gradients $G$ benefit most from placement in CXL memory. Taken together, these findings motivate the multi‑AIC striping approach, which splits each GPU process’s data across all available AICs, preventing concurrent traffic from piling onto a single card and relieving the observed bandwidth bottleneck. When the system contains more AICs than GPUs—for instance, one GPU paired with two AICs—the data is striped across all cards so transfers can harness their combined bandwidth. Figure~\ref{fig:method_example}(b) illustrates the arrangement for a two‑GPU, two‑AIC configuration.

Multi‑AIC striping extends naturally to CPU‑intensive phases. If the full‑precision optimizer states $O$ exceed local DRAM capacity, the data can be partitioned across both DRAM and the available AICs. During the optimizer step, the CPU accesses these partitions in parallel, drawing on the aggregate bandwidth of the local DRAM plus the CXL fabric. Figure~\ref{fig:method_example}(c) shows such a layout with two AICs. By distributing traffic across multiple cards, the strategy capitalizes on CXL’s aggregate bandwidth, improves scalability in GPU transfer phases, and alleviates memory‑capacity pressure during CPU‑bound steps.

%Although CXL AICs allow the system to expand memory capacity easily, effectively utilizing multiple AICs is crucial. Our analysis in Section~\ref{sec:analysis:gpu_transfer} highlighted significant bandwidth contention when multiple GPUs concurrently access the same CXL AIC. Furthermore, the CXL-aware Memory Allocation strategy (Section~\ref{sec:design:allocation}) designates CXL-attached memory primarily for latency-tolerant GPU transfer data (e.g., checkpointed activations $A$, parameters $P$, gradients $G$). Based on these observations, we propose \textbf{Multi-AIC Striping}.

%This strategy partitions the data associated with each GPU process among the available AICs. By distributing the accesses, it prevents multiple GPUs from simultaneously targeting the same AIC, thus mitigating the identified bandwidth contention issue. In scenarios where there are more AICs than GPUs (e.g., one GPU and two AICs), data is striped across all available AICs to leverage their aggregate bandwidth during data transfers. Figure~\ref{fig:method_example}(b) illustrates this concept for a two-GPU, two-AIC configuration.

%\textbf{Multi-AIC Striping} can also be applied when the data required for CPU-intensive tasks, such as the scenario in which the full-precision optimizer states $O$ exceed the local DRAM capacity. In this case, the data can be partitioned and striped across both local DRAM and the available CXL AICs. During the optimizer step, the CPU can then concurrently access data from all locations, using the combined bandwidth of the local DRAM and the CXL AICs. An example of this is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:method_example}(c), where the optimizer states are striped across the local DRAM and two AICs.

