\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Fixed domain curve counts}

Let $X$ be a smooth complex projective variety. 
Gromov--Witten theory is concerned with counting holomorphic maps from complex curves to $X$. A rigorous definition of such counts involves moduli spaces of stable maps introduced by Gromov and Kontsevich. Given $g, n \in \N_0$ and $A \in H_2(X,\Z)$, let $\cM_{g,n}(X;A)$ be the moduli space of stable maps of arithmetic genus $g$ and homology class $A$, with $n$ marked points. This space may be highly singular and non-reduced; nevertheless, it carries a virtual fundamental class
\[
    [\cM_{g,n}(X;A)]^{\mathrm{vir}} \in H_*(\cM_{g,n}(X;A), \Q), 
\]
which can be paired against cohomology classes in $\cM_{g,n}(X;A)$ to produce rational numbers.  A natural way to construct such classes is to consider the map
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn: tau map}
    \tau \colon \cM_{g,n}(X;A) \to \cM_{g,n} \times X^n,
\end{equation}
which encodes the stabilization of the domain of a stable map and its values at the $n$ marked points. See \autoref{def: stabilization morphism} for details. Here and throughout, we assume that $2g - 2 + n > 0$ so that $\cM_{g,n}$ is a Deligne–Mumford stack. Given $\gamma \in H^*( \cM_{g,n} \times X^n, \Q)$, the corresponding Gromov--Witten invariant is defined by pairing the virtual fundamental class against $\tau^*\gamma$. 

In general, this invariant differs from the number of genus $g$ holomorphic curves in $X$ representing class $A$ and satisfying geometric constraints imposed by $\gamma$. It is an interesting but difficult question under what conditions on $X$, $A$, $g$, $n$, and $\gamma$ the Gromov--Witten invariant and the geometric count agree; if this is the case, we say that the invariant is enumerative. 

In this article, we deal with problem and focus on the case when $\gamma$ is Poincaré dual to a point:
\[
    \gamma = \mathrm{PD}[\mathrm{pt}] \in H^{\mathrm{top}}( \cM_{g,n} \times X^n, \Q).
\]
The degree of $\gamma$ is equal to the virtual dimension of the moduli space if and only if
\begin{equation}\label{eqn: num constraint}
   \langle c_1(X), A \rangle = r(n + g - 1) \quad\text{where } r = \dim_\C X.
\end{equation} 
The resulting Gromov--Witten invariant can be intepreted as the virtual count of stable maps from genus $g$ nodal curves with a \emph{fixed} stabilization passing through a \emph{fixed} collection of $n$ points in $X$, and representing homology class $A$. Thus, it is known as the \textbf{fixed domain Gromov--Witten invariant} or  as the \textbf{virtual Tevelev degree}, and denoted by 
\[
    \vTev_{g,n}(X;A) = \langle [\cM_{g,n}(X;A)], \tau^*  \mathrm{PD}[\mathrm{pt}] \rangle \in \Q.
\]

Fixed domain curve counts  appear in many contexts in algebraic geometry and mathematical physics. Such counts for Grassmannians are computed using the celebrated Intriligator--Vafa formula \cite{bdw}, partially proved in \cite{st,bdw, B1}, and fully in \cite{mo} using Quot schemes. The equivalence with the formulation in terms of stable maps was established in \cite{mop}. See also \cite{BP} and \cite{Cela} for a modern treatment dealing also with complete intersections. The systematic study of fixed domain curve counts for general targets began with \cite{CPS}, which was motivated by work on scattering amplitudes in mathematical physics \cite{tev}. The work \cite{CPS} introduced the term Tevelev degrees to refer to the actual count of such curves. The results of \cite{CPS} then sparked a series of subsequent studies \cite{BLLRST, BP, Cela, CL2, CL, FL, lian_pr, LP} connecting the problem to other areas of mathematics such as interpolation problems and the semistability of the tangent bundle of $X$ \cite{cl_hirzebruch}, and tropical geometry \cite{CIL, cd}.

Lian and Pandharipande showed that when the degree \eqref{eqn: num constraint}, or equivalently, the number of marked points $n$, is large, there is also a \textbf{geometric Tevelev degree} defined as follows. Consider the restriction of $\tau$ to the locus of maps with smooth domains
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq: smooth domain tau}
    \M_{g,n}(X;A) \to \M_{g,n} \times X^n. 
\end{equation}
It is shown in \cite{LP} that for $n \geq g+1$, the general fiber of this map consists of finitely many non-stacky reduced points, and the geometric Tevelev degree $\Tev_{g,n}(X;A) \in \N_0$ is defined as the cardinality of the general fiber. 


As is the case for many other Gromov--Witten invariants, the virtual Tevelev degrees are not always enumerative:
\[
    \vTev_{g,n}(X;A) \neq \Tev_{g,n}(X;A).
\]
Indeed, \cite{BP} exhibits a case in which $\mathsf{vTev}^X_{g,n,A} < 0$ whereas, by definition, $\Tev_{g,n}(X;A) \geq 0$. Interestingly, however, in all known examples $\vTev_{g,n}(X;A)$ is an integer. Moreover, based on a series of examples, Lian and Pandharipande speculated that the virtual Tevelev degrees of Fano varieties are enumerative when $n$ is large \cite{LP}.

\begin{conjecture}[Lian--Pandharipande]
    For every $g$ and $r$ there exists a constant $C = C(g,r)$ with the following property. If $X$ is a smooth Fano variety of complex dimension $r$, $n\geq C$, and $A \in H_2(X,\Z)$ satisfies \eqref{eqn: num constraint}, then
    \[
        \vTev_{g,n}(X;A) = \Tev_{g,n}(X;A).
    \]  
\end{conjecture}

When $r = 1$, so that $X = \mathbb{P}^1$, the conjecture can be verified directly. More generally, for $X = \mathbb{P}^r$ and $A = dH$, where $H$ is the hyperplane class, and $d \geq rg + r$, we have
\[
    \vTev_{g,n}(\mathbb{P}^r, dH) = \Tev_{g,n}(\mathbb{P}^r, dH)  = (r+1)^g;
\]
see \cite{FL} or \cite{BP,LP}. Geometric Tevelev degrees of projective spaces in low degrees are also known: see \cite{CPS} for closed formulas in the case of $\mathbb{P}^1$, and \cite{lian_pr} for a formula for $\mathbb{P}^r$ expressed in terms of Schubert calculus.
% Exact computations are known for both the virtual and geometric Tevelev degrees of $\mathbb{P}^r$. One always has $\mathsf{vTev}^{\mathbb{P}^r}_{g,n,d} = (r+1)^g$, while the formulas for the geometric counts are more involved. See \cite[Theorem~1]{CPS} for closed formulas in the case of $\mathbb{P}^1$, and \cite[Theorem~1.1]{Lian24} for Schubert calculus formulas in higher dimensions.
More generally, the Lian--Pandharipande conjecture is true if $X = G/P$ is a homogeneous space for a linear algebraic group \cite{LP}. 
Finally, it holds for $r=2$, i.e. for Del Pezzo surfaces \cite{CL2}, and for low-degree Fano complete intersections \cite{LP,BLLRST}.
%In these cases as well, explicit computations are available and show that the two degrees coincide. See \cite[Theorems~1.4 and~1.5]{BP} and \cite[Theorem~10]{Cela} for the virtual counts, and \cite[Theorem~12]{Lian2025} for the geometric ones.

However, the general statement of the conjecture was recently disproved in the paper \cite{BLLRST} which provided explicit counterexamples such as Fano splitting projective bundles over $\mathbb{P}^k$ with $k > 1$, or certain Fano hypersurfaces in projective space containing special divisors, for instance, the Fermat hypersurface.

\subsection{A symplectic Lian--Pandharipande conjecture}

The present article rectifies the Lian--Pandharipande conjecture by providing an enumerative interpretation of the virtual Tevelev degrees in symplectic geometry. Henceforth, let $(X,\omega)$ be a compact symplectic manifold. Since the conjecture is known for $r=2$, we assume that $\dim_\R X = 2r \geq 6$. The symplectic analogue of the Fano condition is that $(X,\omega)$ is \textbf{positive} in the sense that
\[
    \langle c_1(X,\omega), A \rangle > 0
\]
for every $A \in H_2(X,\Z)$ which is positive with respect to $\omega$, that is: $\langle [\omega], A \rangle > 0$. Here $c_1(X,\omega)$ is the first Chern class of the almost complex manifold $(X,J)$ for any choice $J$ of an almost complex structure on $X$ compatible with $\omega$.

Denote by $\cJ$ the infinite-dimensional Banach manifold of all compatible almost complex structures on $X$ of regularity $C^k$, for a fixed $k\geq 1$. For every $J \in \cJ$, the Gromov--Witten invariants and virtual Tevelev degrees can be constructed using $J$-holomorphic rather than holomorphic map. Indeed, the moduli space of stable $J$-holomorphic maps $\cM_{g,n}(X,J;A)$ carries a virtual fundamental class in the Borel--Moore homology and the Gromov--Witten invariant are defined as before and independent of $J \in \cJ$ \cite{pardon}; in particular, the virtual Tevelev degrees $\vTev_{g,n}(X,\omega;A)$ are defined. The algebraic framework discussed earlier is a special case: a smooth Fano variety equipped with the Fubini--Study form is a positive symplectic manifold and the algebraic moduli space and virtual fundamental class agree with the symplectic ones when $J$ is integrable.

Unlike the virtual Tevelev degree, the geometric Tevelev degree has no obvious analogue for a non-integrable $J$. For a general symplectic manifold, the general fibers of the map \eqref{eq: smooth domain tau} are a priori not necessarily finite and even if they are, their cardinality might depend on $J$.

The main result of this paper shows that if $(X,\omega)$ is positive, $n$ is large, and $J$ is generic, then the geometric Tevelev degree is well-defined and an analogue of the Lian--Pandharipande conjecture holds. Here by generic we mean: chosen from a countable intersection of open dense subsets of $\cJ$; note that such an intersection is dense by Baire's category theorem.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: main 0}
    Let $(X,\omega)$ be a positive symplectic manifold of real dimension $2r \geq 6$. There exists a constant $C = C(r,g)$ with the following property. If $n \geq C$, then for every $A \in H_2(X,\Z)$ satisfying \eqref{eqn: num constraint}, a generic $J \in \cJ$, and a generic point
    $\zeta \in \M_{g,n} \times X^n$, the fiber $\tau_J^{-1}(\zeta)$ of
    \[
        \tau_J \colon \cM_{g,n}(X,J;A) \to \cM_{g,n} \times X^n
    \]
    is finite and consists of simple maps with smooth domain, in particular, it is contained in $\M_{g,n}(X,J;A)$. The signed count of points in the fiber,
    \begin{equation}
        \label{eqn: symplectic geometric Tevelev}
        \Tev_{g,n}(X,\omega; A) \coloneq \sum_{[u] \in \tau_J^{-1}(\zeta)} \mathrm{sign}(u),
    \end{equation}
    does not depend on such $J$ and $\zeta$ and agrees with the virtual Tevelev degree:
    \[
        \vTev_{g,n}(X,\omega; A) = \Tev_{g,n}(X,\omega; A).
    \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
    If $(X,\omega)$ is a positive symplectic manifold of $\dim_\R X \geq 6$ and $n \geq C(g,r)$, then the virtual Tevelev degree $\vTev_{g,n}(X,\omega;A)$ is an integer. 
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
    In the situation described in \autoref{thm: main 0}, the geometric Tevelev degree \eqref{eqn: symplectic geometric Tevelev} has a clear enumerative interpretation. Indeed, the points of $\tau^{-1}(\zeta)$ correspond to $J$-holomorphic curves in $X$ with a fixed complex structure and passing through a fixed collection of $n$ points in $X$. (Moreover, standard transversality arguments show that for a generic $J$, all such curves are pairwise disjoint embedded submanifolds.)

    There is a general idea, inspired by the work of Gopakumar and Vafa in string theory \cite{gopakumar}, that in certain special cases there exist integer-valued curve-counting invariants, called the  \textbf{BPS invariants}, which are more geometric in nature than the Gromov--Witten invariants, yet carry the same information. For symplectic manifolds of real dimension six, these are conjecturally related to the Gromov--Witten invariants via the celebrated Gopakumar--Vafa formula \cite{ionel}.

    If the geometric Tevelev degree can be defined for arbitrary positive symplectic manifolds and homology classes, it would be a natural candidate for the 'fixed domain version' of the BPS invariant. In that case, \autoref{thm: main 0} could be interpreted as the equality of the fixed domain BPS and Gromov--Witten invariants in high degree. It is an interesting question whether such a geometric invariant can be defined in low degrees, and, if so, whether there exists a universal formula relating it to the fixed domain Gromov--Witten invariants.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
    While the result is stated for the space $\cJ$ of $C^k$ almost complex structure, a standard argument by Taubes allows one to replace it by the space of $C^\infty$ structures \cite{mcduff}. 
\end{remark}

\subsection{A generalization: codimension estimates of strata}

\autoref{thm: main 0} is a consequence of a much more general result which analyzes the subsets 
\[
    \M_\G(X,J;A) \subset \cM_{g,n}(X,J;A)
\] 
consisting of stable maps whose domain is modelled on an $n$-marked genus $g$ graph $\G$. Like the full moduli space, these subspaces are not, in general, smooth and of expected dimension. For example, if $\G = T_{g,n}$ is the graph consisting of a single vertex of genus $g$ with $n$ markings, the corresponding subset is $\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(X,J;A)$, the space of maps from smooth domains, which can be further decomposed into the spaces of multiple covers and simple maps, see \autoref{def: simple}. Similarly, for a general graph $\G$ we have the open subset
$$
\M_\G^*(X,J;A) \subset \M_\G(X,J;A)
$$
of simple maps, which is smooth and of expected dimension for a generic $J$. The complement of this subset is a union of strata of different dimensions, often higher than the expected dimension.

Since we are only interested in properties of $\cM_\G(X,J;A)$ for a generic $J$, it is convenient to consider the universal moduli space over $\cJ$:
\[
    \cM_\G(X,\cJ;A) \to \cJ.
\]
By combining the map $\tau$ from \eqref{eqn: tau map} with the projection to $\cJ$, define 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn: map tau}
\tau_{\G}:\M_{\mathsf{\Gamma}}(X,A,\cJ) \to X^n \times \cM_{g,n} \times \cJ;
\end{equation}
\autoref{thm: main 0} can be then proved by controlling the codimension of the image of $\tau_\G$. Recall that a subset $S$ of a Banach orbifold $B$ is said to have \textbf{codimension $\geq c$} if there exists a Banach orbifold $E$ and a $C^1$ Fredholm map $f: E \to B$ of index at most $-c$ such that $S \subset f(E)$. By the Sard--Smale theorem, if $c > 0$, then the complement of $S$ is contained in a countable intersection of open dense subsets.


\begin{theorem}\label{thm: main} 
Let $(X,\omega)$ be a positive symplectic manifold of real dimension $2r \geq 6$. There exists a constant $C = C(r,g)$ with the following property. If $n \geq C$, then for every $A \in H_2(X,\Z)$ satisfying \eqref{eqn: num constraint} and an $n$-marked genus $g$ graph $\G$, 
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] if $\mathsf{\Gamma} \neq T_{g,n}$, then the image of $\tau_{\mathsf{\Gamma}}$ has positive codimension;
    \item[(ii)] if $\mathsf{\Gamma} = T_{g,n}$, then the image under $\tau_\G$ of the space of multiple covers
    \[
     \mathcal{M}_{\mathsf{\Gamma}}(X, A, \cJ) \setminus \mathcal{M}_{\mathsf{\Gamma}}^*(X, A, \cJ)
    \]
    has positive codimension.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Strategy and difficulties of the proof}

The fact that none of the singular strata of $\cM_{g,n}(X,J;A)$ contribute to the Tevelev degree is surprising and relies crucially on the assumption that the marked domain curve is fixed. Indeed, the analogous statement without this constraint is known to fail for Fano varieties, where the corresponding invariants are not necessarily integers \cite{GP99}. Even when the complex structure of the domain is fixed but the markings are not, the statement remains false \cite{Ionel98, Zinger03}.

The main ideas behind the proof, and the organization of the paper, are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}[(i)]

\item The first step, discussed in \autoref{sec: stratification}, is to further stratify the moduli spaces of maps by introducing the notion of an \textbf{augmented graph} $\cG$, which enhances a dual graph $\G$ of the domain curve with certain additional combinatorial data. This data records, among other things, the degree of the stable map on each component, the homology class of the underlying simple map, and whether two components have the same image. Moreover, we record an auxiliary collection of weights $\bm$ associated with each vertex and define the associated \textbf{weighted homology class} $[\G, \underline{\mathsf{m}}]$ that keeps track of these multiplicities.  

\item In \autoref{sec: simplification process} we describe a \textbf{simplification process}: given a map $u$ modelled on an augmented graph $\cG$, we define a simple map $u^s$ modelled on a new augmented graph $\cG^s$. This construction is inspired by the procedure of McDuff–Salamon \cite[Section~6.1]{mcduff}, but is substantially more refined. In particular, the genus of the domain curve may increase during simplification, and the homology class of the map may change. However, the weighted homology class remains unchanged.

As an example, suppose $u \colon C \to X$ is a $J$-holomorphic map from a curve $C$ with three components $T_1,T_2$ and $C_0$, as on the left in \autoref{fig:simplification-step}


